---
title: "The ticker"
description: "The 60-second loop: everything that happens without a user action, and the three different date gates that must not be collapsed into one."
---

`src/bot/ticker.ts` is 392 lines, the largest file in the project, and it is where
most confusing production behaviour comes from. It is one `setInterval` at 60
seconds, started in `main.ts`, doing three jobs.

Read this chapter before debugging anything about the pinned message, the Monday
post, or reminder timing.

## One tick

```{mermaid}
%%| label: fig-tick
%%| fig-cap: "One tick. The three gates are drawn separately because they stop at different dates."
flowchart TD
  S(["every 60s"]) --> RUN{"previous tick<br/>still running?"}
  RUN -->|yes| SKIP["warn + skip"]
  RUN -->|no| CAL["calendar(): today, yesterday,<br/>weekStart, hour"]
  CAL --> W{"inWindow<br/>OR previous week<br/>in competition?"}
  W -->|neither| DONE(["return"])
  W -->|yes| REM{"inWindow?"}
  REM -->|yes| SEND["sendDueReminders()<br/><i>own try/catch</i>"]
  REM -->|no| CHATS
  SEND --> CHATS["listChats()"]
  CHATS --> PIN{"inWindow AND<br/>15 min since<br/>last refresh?"}
  PIN -->|yes| Q["standings x2,<br/>once for all chats"]
  PIN -->|no| LOOP
  Q --> LOOP["for each chat<br/><i>own try/catch</i>"]
  LOOP --> RP["refreshPin, if this<br/>is a refresh tick"]
  RP --> MP{"shouldPostMonday?"}
  MP -->|yes| POST["sendMondayPost"]
```

## The three gates, and why they differ

This is the part that is genuinely subtle. The ticker has three time conditions
and they stop at **three different dates**.

| Job | Gate | Stops when |
|---|---|---|
| Pin refresh | `inWindow` **and** 15 minutes elapsed | `COMPETITION_END` |
| Monday post | `previousWeekInCompetition(weekStart)` | one week past `COMPETITION_END` |
| Reminder pass | `inWindow` | `COMPETITION_END` |

::: {.callout-important}
## The pin's gate must stay the tighter one

`COMPETITION_END` is a Sunday, so the final week's result falls due on the Monday
**after** the window closes. That is why the Monday post runs one week longer than
everything else: otherwise the competition's actual final result is the one result
never announced.

The trap: outside the window, `standings(weekStart, today)` covers a week the
competition does not, so it is an **all-zero** "This week" table. Relaxing the
pin's gate to match the post's would overwrite the frozen final standings in every
guild chat with a table of zeroes, in front of the whole competition, at the exact
moment nobody is watching the logs any more.

This is why `refreshPins` carries `inWindow` rather than the function returning on
one relaxed condition.
:::

The reminder pass has its own placement rule, and it is wrong in two different
ways if moved:

- **Inside the window gate**, so reminders stop with the competition rather than
  asking people for a week afterwards to log days FR-26 refuses to store.
- **Outside the 15-minute pin branch.** Nested there, a reminder would land on a
  quarter-hour lattice, and a user whose window opened at 20:01 would wait until
  20:15.

## The running guard

```ts
let running = false;
```

`setInterval` starts a new tick every 60 seconds regardless of whether the
previous one finished, and grammY's default per-call API timeout is 500 seconds.
So a single slow Telegram call can leave several ticks in flight at once.

Concurrent ticks each read `chat.lastMondayWeek` before any of them has called
`recordMondayPost`, so each independently decides the Monday post is owed and each
sends it. **The `chats.last_monday_week` ledger guarantees exactly-once across
restarts, not across overlapping ticks in the same process.** Both guarantees are
needed and neither alone was sufficient.

A skipped tick is not work lost: `shouldPostMonday` and the pin refresh both
operate on live state, so the next tick a minute later picks up exactly where the
skipped one would have started.

::: {.callout-note}
## What this does not protect against

A second OS process running the same ticker against the same database would still
double-post, because `running` is per-process memory rather than a database lock.
That case is ruled out at the deployment level (NFR-2: one machine, one process),
not in code.
:::

## The pinned message

FR-19. **It is edited, never resent**, and the edit is skipped entirely when the
rendered text is unchanged. Resending would notify a whole guild every 15 minutes,
which is the precise opposite of what the requirement asks for.

Skipping the unchanged edit removes the API call rather than making it and
swallowing Telegram's 400. Overnight, when nobody logs, the refresh does nothing at
all.

The two conditions in that check are separate on purpose:

```ts
const unchanged = chat.pinnedText === text;
if (unchanged && !chat.pinFailed) return;
```

A chat whose text has not changed but whose pin previously **failed** still has
work to do: retry the pin. Editing it with identical text to get there would
produce exactly the 400 the check exists to avoid.

`pinned_text` is stored for this comparison only. It is a rendered string and is
never read back as data.

**A refused pin is not a failure.** The pin is the requirement, but the live number
is the value, so the message is still sent and still updates, with a line asking
for admin rights appended, and the pin is retried on every refresh until it takes.
That way a board's fix applies without anyone restarting anything.

## Three failures, three different recoveries

The error handling here is unusually specific, and the distinctions are all
earned.

| Detector | Matches | Recovery |
|---|---|---|
| `isGone` | 403, or the supergroup-upgrade 400 | Delete the `chats` row. The chat is gone for good |
| `isMessageGone` | "message to edit not found" | Null `pinned_message_id`, so the next refresh sends and pins a fresh message |
| `isSupergroupUpgrade` | the upgrade 400 alone | Rethrown from `tryPin`, where a bare 403 must not be trusted |

::: {.callout-warning}
## Why `isGone` is deliberately narrow

A generic "chat not found" is Telegram's catch-all for "this id did not resolve",
which a transient lookup failure also produces. Treating it as gone-for-good would
delete a perfectly good chat's row on a false positive.

Worse, a later rebind resets `last_monday_week` to the current week, which would
silently suppress that week's Monday post with no signal anywhere. A bot actually
removed from a chat gets a 403, which stays in the set, so nothing real goes
unhandled.
:::

The `tryPin` case is the subtlest. `pinChatMessage`'s 403 is Telegram's ordinary
answer to "the bot lacks the pin permission", which is exactly the recoverable
state the admin-request line exists for, **not** a gone chat. Trusting `isGone`
there would unbind a chat that merely lacks one admin right. So only the
supergroup-upgrade case is rethrown.

The honest consequence, recorded rather than hidden: a bot genuinely removed from a
chat is still caught, but **not always on that tick**. When the pin was already
failing and the standings text has not changed, `refreshPin` makes no other
Telegram call, so the 403 arrives in `tryPin` and is swallowed as a missing
permission. Recovery is delayed, not lost. Worst case is roughly a week wrongly
bound, during which the only cost is edit attempts against a chat that is gone.

## Isolation

Every chat's work is wrapped in its own `try`/`catch`, so one chat's failure does
not cost the other eight their pin refresh and Monday post. The reminder pass gets
its own guard for the same reason, and the batch's per-user isolation lives inside
`sendDueReminders`.

Even the recovery paths are guarded. `unbindChat` is wrapped, because an error
there (a dropped database connection, say) would escape the per-chat catch and
abandon every remaining chat that tick, which is exactly what the isolation
exists to prevent.

## One known gap, on shutdown

`main.ts` stops the ticker before the bot, so no new tick starts while `bot.stop()`
drains in-flight updates. `clearInterval` cannot abort a tick already running,
though.

If the process is killed between `sendMessage` and `recordMondayPost` inside
`sendMondayPost`, that week's Monday message is **re-posted on the next boot**,
because the ledger write never landed. This is documented in `main.ts:20-27`
rather than solved, since solving it means a transaction spanning a Telegram call.

## Reminder sends

`sendDueReminders` in `src/bot/reminders.ts` is called once per tick. It reads the
due set (capped at 25, paced at 25 per minute against Telegram's 30-per-second
broadcast limit), renders the check-in message **once for the whole batch**, and
sends.

Two errors are special:

- **403**: the user has blocked the bot. Recorded via `setBlocked`, and
  `dueReminders` never selects that row again until the user messages the bot.
- **429**: rate limited. The pass **stops entirely** rather than continuing, because
  this is Telegram saying the batch itself is the problem. The remaining users stay
  due and the next tick retries them, still inside the grace window.

`recordReminder` is called **only after** Telegram accepted the message. A send that
succeeds and then fails to record re-sends on the next tick, bounded by the grace
window. That gap is accepted rather than solved, for the same reason as the Monday
post's: the alternative is a transaction spanning a network call.
